@meta
id: 29-13
title: A fuzzy finder in thirty lines
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: fuzzy-finder, packadd
par: 33
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-P, the feature you will miss most
open: notes.md
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
Third plugin, shipped with the lesson:

    pack/dojo/start/files/plugin/files.vim

Thirty lines. It defines one command, `:Files`, which takes a scrap of a
path and opens the file that best matches it. `:Files opr` opens
`src/domain/order/order.provider.ts`. The letters have to appear in the
path in that order and nothing else has to be true — that is what
"fuzzy" means, and it is the entire idea behind fzf, Telescope, Ctrl-P
and every finder you have used.

The ranking is `matchfuzzy()`, which vim has had built in since 8.2. The
plugin lists the files with `globpath()` and hands the list to it. There
is no fuzzy-matching algorithm in this plugin because there did not need
to be one.

`notes.md` has the ticket. Do what it says. You should not need to type a
full path, and you should not need `:e` with a directory tree in it.

Leave `notes.md` alone.

@keys
| `:Files {fragment}` | open the best match for `{fragment}` |
| `:Files ordrep` | letters in order, anywhere in the path |
| `:Files <Tab>` | complete on the command line, best match first |

@hints
The file you want is `src/domain/order/order.repository.ts`. Pick letters
from it that are unlikely to appear in that order anywhere else.
--
`:Files ordrep` gets you there. Then it is an ordinary edit and an
ordinary `:wq`.
--
`:Files ordrep` `Enter`, `/take` `Enter`, `f2` `r5`, `:wq` `Enter`.

@solution
    :Files ordrep<Enter>  /take<Enter>  f2  r5  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes, of which six were the file name.

Read the plugin. The interesting half is one line:

    return a:pat ==# '' ? all : matchfuzzy(all, a:pat)

and the rest is housekeeping: list the files, drop the directories, strip
the leading `./`, skip the package directory so the finder does not offer
you its own source. A real finder skips `.git`, `node_modules` and
`dist` the same way, usually by asking `git ls-files` for the list
instead of walking the disk — which is faster on a big repo and respects
`.gitignore` for free.

What you now have is a fair comparison. Chapter 18 gave you `:find` with
`set path=.,**`, which is vim's own answer and completes on a prefix.
This is the other answer: match anywhere, rank by closeness. The second
one is nicer to type and the first one needs no plugin at all. Try both
for a week before you decide you need fzf.

One warning that applies to every finder you will install: they are the
plugins most likely to fight your muscle memory, because they take over
`Ctrl-P`, `Ctrl-N` and often `Ctrl-J`/`Ctrl-K` inside their own window.
Read the mapping section of the help before you install one, not after.

@start pack/dojo/start/files/plugin/files.vim
" dojo-files.vim - a fuzzy file finder in thirty lines.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   :Files {pattern}   open the file whose path best matches {pattern}
"   :Files <Tab>       cycle the candidates on the command line
"
" The ranking is matchfuzzy(), which vim has had built in since 8.2. The
" letters you type have to appear in the path in order, and nothing else.
" `opr` finds order.provider.ts. That is the whole trick fzf sells.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_files')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_files = 1

function! s:index() abort
  let all = split(globpath('.', '**/*'), "\n")
  call filter(all, 'filereadable(v:val)')
  call map(all, 'substitute(v:val, "^\\./", "", "")')
  " A real finder skips .git, node_modules and build output. This one skips
  " the package directory, so the list is the project and nothing else.
  return filter(all, 'v:val !~# "^pack/"')
endfunction

function! s:matches(pat) abort
  let all = s:index()
  return a:pat ==# '' ? all : matchfuzzy(all, a:pat)
endfunction

function! s:complete(lead, line, pos) abort
  return s:matches(a:lead)
endfunction

function! s:files(pat) abort
  let hits = s:matches(a:pat)
  if empty(hits)
    echohl ErrorMsg
    echo 'Files: nothing matches ' . a:pat
    echohl NONE
    return
  endif
  execute 'edit ' . fnameescape(hits[0])
endfunction

command! -nargs=? -complete=customlist,s:complete Files call s:files(<q-args>)

@start notes.md
# ORD-503

The order list endpoint pages twenty at a time. Support are paging
through refund queues by hand and twenty is too few.

Change the page size in the order repository from 20 to 50. Nothing else
changes; the customer repository keeps its own default.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async list(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId } });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  totalMinor: number;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start src/domain/customer/customer.repository.ts
export class CustomerRepository {
  public async findMany(args: FindManyArgs) {
    return this.prisma.customer.findMany({ ...args, take: 20 });
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    return this.client.post('/refunds', { orderId });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(args: FindManyArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({ ...args, take: 20 });
  }

  public async findOne(args: FindOneArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findFirst(args);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(args: FindManyArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({ ...args, take: 50 });
  }

  public async findOne(args: FindOneArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findFirst(args);
  }
}
